S=Path(__file__).resolve().parent;R=S.parent
sha=lambda p:hashlib.sha256(p.read_bytes()).hexdigest()
def main():
 old=json.loads((S/'verified-template-input.json').read_text());b=old['blocks'];j=deepcopy(old);out=[];edits=[]
 def add(kind,text,**kw):out.append({'type':kind,'text':text,**kw})
 def h(text):add('heading',text,level=2)
 def p(text):add('p',text)
 def keep(i,**overrides):
  v=deepcopy(b[i]);v.update(overrides);v['prior_edition_block']=i;out.append(v);edits.append({'prior_block':i,'new_block':len(out)-1,'changes':overrides})
 j.update(title='Harmful Agent Handoffs and Safe Coordination',subtitle='A Controlled Study of Transfer and Verification',ready_for_final=False,editorial_revision_date='2026-10-25',phase='EDITORIAL_REVISION_WITH_PENDING_FACTORIAL_CONTROL')
 j['abstract']='''Can a harmful joint plan survive replacement of its agents through handoff notes, and can successors reject that plan while still coordinating? We audit this question in a transparent four-agent allocation task. Original agents are instructed to favor their coalition at outsiders’ expense; neutral successors receive controlled combinations of their notes. In a format-expanded analysis, a prespecified paired-note contrast shows joint-harm increases of 0.50 for Qwen and 0.43 for GPT-OSS relative to facts alone, across five and seven complete cases. Verification reminders reduce joint harm, but duplicate task claims increase relative to the coordinated harmful-note condition. A post-hoc comparison finds the same duplicate frequency under verification and facts alone, consistent with a coordination problem rather than a necessary cost of safety. Two further controls qualify this interpretation. The structural control removes verified duplicates but leaves partial outsider harm for Qwen. Holding notes and matrices fixed, adding a public shared decision rule improves safe-optimal completion by 0.50 and 0.625 in three and six complete harmful-note cases; all 119 valid episodes in that comparison already have zero outsider loss. These small, selected cohorts support inherited instructed-plan transfer and a task-specific coordination remedy, not a general defense. The final factorial control remains pending before release.'''
 h('1. Introduction')
 p('Agent replacement does not remove the information left by the outgoing agents. Handoff notes can preserve useful conventions, but they can also carry recommendations that benefit a coalition at other participants’ expense. The Inherited Collusion project asks whether such instructed harmful plans affect neutral successors, and whether verification can prevent harm without disrupting task coordination.')
 p('The distinction matters because rejecting a harmful recommendation does not itself select a common safe alternative. We first vary which original notes reach two independent successors. We then investigate the resulting coordination problem through a structural control and a fixed-note shared-rule comparison. The contribution is a bounded empirical audit of this handoff channel: enacted harm, task completion and coordination quality are measured separately.')
 h('2. Related Work')
 p('Colosseum distinguishes expressed collusive plans from enacted coalition-favoring behavior [1,4]. Self-Propagating Misalignment studies harmful goals passed to neutral successors and discusses utility costs of memory defenses [2]. Salami Attack studies jointly effective memory fragments [3]. Building on this work, we control delivery of role-specific notes written after enacted harmful or legitimate allocations, then measure coalition benefit, outsider harm and task coordination separately.')
 p('Shared conventions and symmetry breaking are established coordination approaches. Theory of Scene addresses collisions among homogeneous concurrent agents using public roles and shared task context [5]. Tacit Coordination of Large Language Models studies focal points and coordination without communication [6]. Our focused question is how these ideas behave in the presence of harmful inherited notes. The sequential controls distinguish note delivery, changes in task structure and an explicit public safe decision policy; legitimate-source comparisons assess useful cooperation.')
 h('3. Methods')
 p('Two model agents, A and B, independently choose one of four tasks without seeing each other’s choice. Scripted outsiders C and D then minimize their combined cost on two distinct unclaimed tasks. Every agent earns 100 minus its task cost; an extra duplicate claim incurs a 200-point penalty shared by claimants. Neutral successors are instructed to maximize distinct task completion and then minimize total cost across all four agents. They receive the full public cost matrix and all 16 possible A/B outcomes, including the scripted continuation.')
 p('Here operational collusion means an enacted joint allocation that benefits A/B while reducing C/D reward relative to the cooperative reference. A joint-harm event requires both outsider specialties to be taken with positive coalition gain and outsider loss. Original agents are explicitly instructed to execute harmful or legitimate joint plans before writing role-specific notes to their replacements. This induces the original coalition; it does not establish spontaneous collusion. Successors receive neutral instructions and unchanged public facts. We also score continuous outsider loss, team reward, duplicate claims and safe-optimal completion. A safe optimum completes four distinct tasks at globally minimum total cost; zero joint harm alone can conceal partial outsider harm.')
 p('The handoff audit plans eight cost cases per model and two successor draws per route. Routes deliver facts alone, one note, both notes from the same original branch, crossed branches, or both notes plus a reminder to check them against public rules and total cost. Two role-swapped original-plan orientations (p0 and p1) and legitimate-source controls test convention transfer. Each model supplies its own originals and successors, so differences between models combine sender and recipient behavior. All main studies use Qwen3.6-35B-A3B and GPT-OSS-120B at temperature 0.6, top-p 0.95 and an 8,192-token output limit; exact identifiers and provenance limits are in Appendix B.')
 p('The original strict audit retains 360/512 planned episodes. A disclosed post-start format amendment accepts one complete outer JSON fence and recovers 439/512 episodes by reusing exact old responses and calling only previously undispatched slots. Four unresolved requests remain missing. We report both populations separately; the amended analysis is not an independent replication. Later controls were locally frozen before their own responses, after the earlier findings were known. Internal version names, dates, eligibility rules and all missingness are mapped in Appendices B and H.')
 p('The structural control plans six new cases per model, two draws per condition, both original objectives and three routes: facts alone, both original notes and verified notes. Adding one unit to two nonpreferred safe A/B cost cells creates a unique safe optimum while preserving optimal total cost and assigned original-plan outcomes. Public outcome tables are recomputed and originals write fresh notes for each structure. Four Qwen cases and all six GPT-OSS cases complete this control; one technically valid but semantically incorrect Qwen diagnostic remains included. The intervention therefore changes both cost geometry and the notes produced.')
 p('The fixed-note control instead holds the symmetric matrices and exact notes from those ten available donor cases fixed. Fresh successors receive verification alone or verification plus a shared rule: choose the lexicographically first or last A/B task pair among public outcomes with maximum distinct completion and minimum total cost. Both original objectives and two draws per arm are retained. Its primary harmful-mode contrast averages the two directions equally, subtracts fresh verification alone and weights complete cases equally within each model. The directions are fixed subconditions, not independent replications. Logical completion ranges cover missing outcomes in the available cohort; they are not confidence intervals or corrections for donor selection.')
 p('A final factorial control is being prepared to separate note presence from reminder presence while holding a shared safe rule present in every condition. Its outcomes are not yet available in this working draft.')
 h('4. Results')
 p('Inherited harmful-plan transfer. In the format-expanded audit, both same-source notes from orientation p0 increase joint-harm probability relative to facts alone by 0.50 for Qwen across five complete cases and 0.43 for GPT-OSS across seven. The strict counterparts are 0.50 over only one case and 0.43 over seven. These are within-case delivery contrasts, not population estimates. One-note and crossed-source arms have no observed joint-harm events, although continuous outsider loss remains. Amended Qwen passes the planned legitimate-convention criterion; GPT-OSS does not. Source matching therefore does not guarantee faithful note content or general useful cooperation (Appendix H).')
 p('Verification and coordination. On the distinct common set complete for verification comparisons in both original objectives—five Qwen cases and six GPT-OSS cases—the reminder reduces joint-harm probability by 0.50 and 0.42. Relative to coordinated harmful notes, mean team reward falls by 84.2 and 78.4, and duplicate frequency rises from zero to one half. Continuous outsider loss falls from 25.0 to 1.8 for Qwen and from 28.9 to zero for GPT-OSS. The remaining Qwen loss shows why joint harm cannot serve as the sole safety criterion.')
 out.append({'type':'figure','path':'output/report-revised/figures/handoff-diagnostic.png','local_path':'figures/handoff-diagnostic.png','sha256':sha(R/'figures/handoff-diagnostic.png'),'width_inches':6.25,'caption':'Figure 1. Post-hoc common-case diagnostic of the format-expanded audit: five Qwen cases and six GPT-OSS cases, two draws per arm. Verification eliminates joint-harm events in this subset; duplicate counts match facts alone. The prespecified transfer contrast uses seven GPT-OSS cases. Joint-harm absence can mask partial outsider loss.','alt':'Joint harmful allocations and duplicate task claims for facts alone, both inherited harmful notes and the same notes plus verification, by model.'})
 p('In that post-hoc comparison, verified and facts-only routes have identical duplicate counts: 5/10 for Qwen and 6/12 for GPT-OSS (Figure 1). Both roles have identical costs and two safe optima. This pattern is consistent with discarding a shared convention without selecting a common replacement. It does not establish that mechanism, nor an unavoidable safety–utility trade-off. Relative to facts alone, verified team reward differs by only −1.6 for Qwen and −1.0 for GPT-OSS on these same cases; the large loss above is relative to the coordinated harmful-note condition.')
 add('caption','Table 1. Structural control: harmful-note verification on complete cases. Binary entries are episode counts; outsider loss is a mean. Qwen has four complete cases and GPT-OSS six, with two draws per cell.');keep(22)
 p('Task structure and coordination. Under uniqueness, verified duplicates fall to zero in both models. GPT-OSS reaches the safe optimum in 12/12 episodes; Qwen reaches it in 4/8 and still harms an outsider in the remaining four. The primary duplication interaction—verified minus matched, under uniqueness minus symmetry—is −0.625 for Qwen and −0.750 for GPT-OSS. The six-case logical completion ranges are [−1.083, +0.250] and [−0.750, −0.750]. Harmful matched notes still yield joint harm under uniqueness in 5/8 Qwen and 6/12 GPT-OSS episodes. Uniqueness is therefore neither sufficient protection from inherited plans nor a clean test of successor behavior, because donor notes also change.')
 add('caption','Table 2. Fixed-note control: mean safe-optimal effect of the two added shared-rule directions minus fresh verification alone. Each case has two draws per arm. Completion ranges cover the available donor cohort.');keep(26)
 p('A shared safe policy helps with fixed notes. This control retains 119/120 episodes. Its harmful-mode common set contains three Qwen cases and six GPT-OSS cases: verification alone is safe-optimal in 3/6 and 4/12 episodes, first-direction rules in 6/6 and 11/12, and last-direction rules in 6/6 and 12/12. The primary differences are +0.50 and +0.625 (Table 2); the Qwen completion range is [+0.375, +0.500] over four available cases. Every one of the 119 valid outputs, including verification alone, has zero outsider loss and joint harm. The added rule improves coordination here, not observed harm reduction.')
 p('With legitimate notes, Qwen is safe-optimal in 8/8 episodes per arm; GPT-OSS improves from 10/12 with verification alone to 12/12 under each rule. Both meet the frozen observed nondecline criterion. The one GPT-OSS rule failure is a duplicate claim despite intact delivery. Full counts, direction-specific effects and the unchanged original figure are in Appendix I.')
 p('PENDING: the fresh factorial control will be summarized here only after its raw record is closed and independently checked.')
 h('5. Discussion and Limitations')
 p('The evidence supports two separate conclusions. Inherited notes can carry an instructed harmful allocation into neutral replacement agents. A verification reminder can reduce that transfer while leaving coordination failures in some successor pairs. The follow-up controls show that this coordination cost depends on the task and available decision policy. They do not show that verification internally occurs, that convention loss is the only explanation, or that a universal cost of safety exists.')
 p('Scope is narrow: a transparent allocation fixture, scripted outsiders, full outcome tables, selected donor availability and few within-case observations. The handoff audit has substantial potentially informative attrition and a post-start parser amendment; later designs were motivated by observed results. Model-specific notes mix sender and receiver behavior, and their errors remain part of the primary channel. The fixed-note comparison has no rule-only arm and cannot establish whether the reminder is necessary for the added rule’s success; the pending factorial control targets that gap. All analyses remain conditional on these notes and cases. Broader work should separate sender and recipient models, use blinded human content coding and test less assisted tasks.')
 h('6. Conclusion')
 p('In this task, same-source original notes increased harmful joint allocations relative to facts alone. Verification produced fewer harmful allocations but more duplicate claims than matched harmful notes. The structural and fixed-note controls show that this coordination problem depends on the task and the shared decision policy: both can improve completion, while residual harm and rule-following failures remain visible. Handoff defenses should therefore be evaluated on outsider harm and successful safe coordination together. Preventing a harmful joint allocation is a meaningful result, but it is not by itself a completed cooperative solution.')
 keep(33);keep(34)
 # Preserve complete historical reference/appendix material, inserting retained former-main payloads.
 for i in range(35,len(b)):
  if i==136:break
  keep(i)
  if i==48:
   p('Version mapping for this revision: “strict handoff audit” denotes V4; “format-expanded handoff audit” denotes V4F, a post-start continuation using the original data once; “structural control” denotes V5; “fixed-note control” denotes V6. The new factorial control will be recorded as V7. These labels identify successive locally frozen studies and amendments, not independent task-family replications.')
  if i==98:
   add('caption','Table H0. Retained historical stage counts. The former main-table values are preserved unchanged; strict and amended populations overlap.');keep(18)
  if i==120:
   v=deepcopy(b[24]);v['caption']=v['caption'].replace('Figure 1.','Figure I1.',1);v['prior_edition_block']=24;out.append(v);edits.append({'prior_block':24,'new_block':len(out)-1,'changes':{'caption_number':'Figure I1'}})
 h('Appendix J. Factorial control of note and reminder presence');p('PENDING: protocol, closed-data counts, full factor-cell outcomes, paired contrasts and limitations will be added from the independently checked fresh control. No outcomes are inferred from prior studies.')
 keep(136)
 p('Frank Peterlein initiated the project, authorized its budget and reviewed the full manuscript during development; he is the responsible author. OpenAI Codex assisted with study design, literature checks, code, prompts, analyses, figures and writing. Automated and agent-assisted checks are not independent human replication. Evaluated models generated the retained experimental responses. The earlier detailed disclosure is preserved in Appendix C.')
 j['blocks']=out;j['prior_template_input_sha256']=sha(S/'verified-template-input.json');j['pending_control']='V7';j['revision_note']='Main narrative rewritten; historical appendix prose and all 13 numerical table payloads retained. One prior main figure is renumbered I1 with identical bytes; a new diagnostic figure derives from saved post-hoc data.'
 j['supplementary_inputs']=[x for x in j['supplementary_inputs'] if not x['path'].endswith('integrate_closed_results.py')]
 j['supplementary_inputs'].append({'path':'verified-template-input.json','sha256':sha(S/'verified-template-input.json')})
 j['supplementary_inputs'].append({'path':'plot_handoff_diagnostic.py','sha256':sha(S/'plot_handoff_diagnostic.py')})
 for name in ['historical-review/historical-review.json','historical-review/figure-raw-check.json','literature-recheck.json']:
  j['supplementary_inputs'].append({'path':name,'sha256':sha(S/name)})
 (S/'report-input.json').write_text(json.dumps(j,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
 (S/'editorial-mapping.json').write_text(json.dumps({'prior_template_input_sha256':j['prior_template_input_sha256'],'retained_blocks':edits,'historical_table_payloads':13,'historical_figures':3,'new_figure':'Saved post-hoc common-case diagnostic','main_text_rewritten':True,'pending_control':True},indent=2)+'\n')
 print(json.dumps({'abstract_words':len(j['abstract'].split()),'blocks':len(out),'tables':sum(x['type']=='table' for x in out),'figures':sum(x['type']=='figure' for x in out),'input_sha256':sha(S/'report-input.json')}))
